exports[`prompt templates > intake.path renders stably (snapshot) 1`] = `
{
  "messages": [
    {
      "content": "They want to learn: Get conversational in German
Why: personal_goal — Partner’s family is in Munich; I want to hold my own at dinner.
Experience: explored — in their words: Two Duolingo streaks that died; I can order food.
Domain: language acquisition
Approach notes: You'll get the most from comprehensible input paired with real output: hear or read German at your level, then produce your own sentences, not just recognize them. Since Munich dinners are the goal, build around spoken, social register — greetings, family talk, food, opinions — over textbook grammar drills. Until we know your level, early sessions will test where you land; if you already know some German, we'll skip basics and go straight to gaps. As you progress, scaffolding (translations, sentence starters) fades and improvisation load increases.
Progression principles: Start with high-frequency social phrases and questions before tackling grammar rules abstractly. · Interleave listening and speaking early so output habits form alongside comprehension. · Sequence topics around real family-dinner scenarios: introductions, food, opinions, small talk.
Pitfalls to design around: Recognizing words when reading feels like fluency but doesn't mean you can produce them aloud. · Overusing English grammar logic causes systematic errors in case endings and word order. · Politeness and small talk phrases get neglected in favor of vocabulary lists, hurting real dinners.
Topics they selected: Family introductions, Talking about food, Sharing opinions politely
Topics shown but not selected: Small talk starters, Du vs Sie, Present tense verbs, Asking questions naturally, German word order, Bavarian everyday phrases, Munich table customs, Telling stories about your day
What would feel like success: I can follow a dinner conversation · I can tell a short story about my week · I feel relaxed speaking with family",
      "role": "user",
    },
  ],
  "system": [
    {
      "cache": true,
      "text": "You are the generation engine inside thinkering, a local-first mobile app that helps adults make steady, real progress on things they want to learn — in the time they actually have (5–15 minute sessions).

How the product works: an Interest is something the user wants to learn. Its Path is an ordered list of Goals — each one a well-scoped unit teachable in one session, carrying named concepts and skills. Each day the Today tab offers three lanes: Next (introduce a new goal), Strengthen (consolidate via retrieval and practice), and Go further (apply learning to the user's life, or extend it deeper/wider). Completing an activity advances the goal's status: not_started → introduced → strengthened → applied. Activities are multi-page interactive documents built from a library of learning strategies; a deterministic scheduler picks the goals — you never decide scheduling, only content.

